  "authoritative_metadata": "manifest.json",
  "title": "Branched twists: a junction crosses wires softly, never sharply",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "A junction of two closed branches at a trisum square is never an exact crossover, but can be a soft one. (1) Per slice (x0, x2) the junction is W(z1, z3) = |Q||R| - Q_row(z1) R_row(z1) - Q_col(z3) R_col(z3) + Q(z1, z3) R(z1, z3), the incidence signs acting as flips of legs. (2) No hard line wire: for nonnegative branch tensors (colouring counts) and any signs, if at some slice W vanishes on every host pair (z1, z3) outside one parity class, then W is nonzero there for at most one value of z1. Hence no junction is a crossover [x0 = x2 + a][z1 = z3 + b]: the square's points can carry a hard wire, its lines cannot. With a signed branch a junction can be an exact crossover. (3) Soft crossings exist: over all pairs of sign classes of the truncated-octahedron page (1786 tensors) and all incidence signs, 256 junctions are crossover-type (zero turning entries), off the matchgate class, with all four straight entries nonzero, for instance (40000, -1112, 3336, -192); cube, prism and Omni(K2,3) pages give only exclusive crossings (zero double entry). Whether soft crossings make counting under branched twists #P-hard, by interpolation, is left open.",
